MLLM description: The House Fly (Diptera: Muscidae) is a small to medium-sized fly, typically measuring between 6-10 mm in length. Its body is slender and elongated, with a pair of large compound eyes that dominate its head. The thorax is divided into three segments, each bearing a pair of legs. The wings are transparent with a slight golden tint, and they are held flat against the body when at rest.

In terms of coloration, House Flies can vary widely. They often have a metallic green or blue sheen on their body, which can appear darker or lighter depending on the angle of light. Some individuals may exhibit a yellowish or reddish hue on the thorax. The legs and antennae are usually dark, contrasting with the lighter-colored body.

Behaviorally, House Flies are known for their habit of landing on food sources, such as fruits and vegetables, where they feed on nectar and pollen. They are also attracted to decaying organic matter and can be found near garbage bins and sewage areas. Their rapid wing movement and buzzing sound make them easily identifiable in flight.

